[s0l0!-header][h1]Using Memory[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

C is designed to be a low-level language that can easily access memory locations and perform memory-related operations. 
For instance, the [b]scanf()[/b] function places the value entered by the user at the location, or [b]address[/b], of the variable. This is accomplished by using the & symbol. 

[s0l0!-header][b]For Example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1705"]int num;
printf("Enter a number: ");

scanf("%d", [b]&num[/b]);

printf("%d", num);[/code]
[b]&num[/b] is the address of variable [b]num[/b].

A memory address is given as a [b]hexadecimal[/b] number. [b]Hexadecimal[/b], or [b]hex[/b], is a base-16 number system that uses digits 0 through 9 and letters A through F (16 characters) to represent a group of four binary digits that can have a value from 0 to 15. 
It's much easier to read a hex number that is 8 characters long for 32 bits of memory than to try to decipher 32 1s and 0s in binary.
The following program displays the memory addresses for variables [b]i[/b] and [b]k[/b]:[code format="c" codeId="1706"]void test(int k);

int main() {
  int i = 0;
    
  printf("The address of i is %x\n", &i);
  test(i);
  printf("The address of i is %x\n", &i);
  test(i);

  return 0;
}

void test(int k) {
  printf("The address of k is %x\n", &k);
}[/code]
In the printf statement, [b]%x[/b] is the hex format specifier. 
Program output varies from run to run, but looks similar to: [code format="raw" ]The address of i is 846dd754
The address of k is 846dd758
The address of i is 846dd754
The address of k is 846dd758[/code]
The address of a variable stays the same from the time it is declared until the end of its scope. 
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

