[s0l0!-header][h1]Pointers in Expressions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Pointers can be used in [b]expressions[/b] just as any variable. Arithmetic operators can be applied to whatever the pointer is pointing to.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="c" codeId="1708"]int x = 5;
int y;
int *p = NULL;
p = &x;

y = *p + 2; /* y is assigned 7 */
y += *p;     /* y is assigned 12 */
*p = y;       /* x is assigned 12 */
(*p)++;      /* x is incremented to 13 */

printf("p is pointing to the value %d\n", *p); [/code]
Note that parentheses are required for the ++ operator to increment the value being pointed to. The same is true when using the -- operator.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

